-- title:
--   Lemma 1 — representation (18) of $V_t(P,R)$ and the formula (19) for $\delta_t$ (corrected: monotone in $R$ for $P\ge s$)
-- statement:
--   Throughout, the standing model of §3.1 holds: $B,\tilde B$ independent Brownian motions with augmented filtrations $\mathcal F\supseteq\mathcal G$, the asset $dX_t=X_t(\mu_t\,dt+\sigma_t\,dB_t)$, the demand component $C$ with $A_T=C_T+\tilde\sigma\tilde B_T$, the two Markov hypotheses, and $\mathbb P^M$ with $d\mathbb P^M/d\mathbb P=Z_T$ (12).
--
--   Suppose Assumptions 4–5 hold and $\mathbb E^M[(C_T)^-]<\infty$. For every $(P,R)$:
--
--   1. the projected payoff $V_t(P,R)=\mathbb E^M[H_T(P,R)\mid\mathcal F_t]$ has a representation
--   $$
--   V_t(P,R)=V_0(P,R)+\int_0^t\xi_s(P,R)\,dX_s+\int_0^t\delta_s(P,R)\,d\tilde B_s\qquad (18)
--   $$
--   with adapted $\xi,\delta$, where $V_0(P,R)=\mathbb E^M[H_T(P,R)]$;
--   2. for every such representation,
--   $$
--   \delta_t(P,R)=\tilde\sigma(P-s)\,\mathbb P^M(A_T\le R\mid\mathcal F_t)\qquad (19)
--   $$
--   for almost every $t\in[0,T]$, almost surely;
--   3. the process (19) is nondecreasing in $P$, and nondecreasing in $R$ whenever $P\ge s$, for every $t\le T$;
--   4. for $t<T$ it is strictly increasing in $P$, and strictly increasing in $R$ whenever $P>s$.
--
--   The inequalities hold almost surely for each fixed pair of arguments. $\delta$ is the unhedgeable part of the payoff and enters the minimal variance (21).
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: $\mathcal F_t$ and $\mathcal G_t$ are the filtrations generated by $(B_t,\tilde B_t)$ and $B_t$ (p.11) → the $\mathbb P$-augmented natural filtrations of $(B,\tilde B)$ and of $B$; reason: martingale representation (Prop. EC.3) holds for the augmented Brownian filtration, and null-set modifications of an adapted process must stay adapted
--   - reading: $(X_t,\mu_t,\sigma_t)$ is Markovian under $\mathbb P$ and $(X_t,\sigma_t)$ is Markovian under $\mathbb P^M$ (pp.11–12) → the simple Markov property on $[0,T]$ with respect to $\mathcal F$: $\mathbb E_Q[g(Y_t)\mid\mathcal F_s]=\mathbb E_Q[g(Y_t)\mid\sigma(Y_s)]$ a.s. for $s\le t\le T$ and bounded measurable $g$; reason: the paper does not define the term; this is the standard definition, relative to the filtration of footnote 4
--   - reading: $c$ the unit production cost, $s$ the unit salvage value, $X_0$ an asset price → $s<c$ and $x_0>0$ in the parameter structure; reason: the newsvendor convention (p.7), implied by Assumption 3 wherever it is assumed; $X_t>0$ is used on p.ec28
--   - encoding: processes on $[0,T]$ → processes indexed by $\mathbb R_{\ge0}$, constrained only for $t\le T$; reason: Mathlib's Brownian motion lives on $\mathbb R_{\ge0}$; values after $T$ never enter
--   - encoding: stochastic integrals $\int\theta\,dX$, $\int b\,d\tilde B$, $\int\eta\,dB$ with almost-sure integrability → the published relation `EthierKurtz.HasBrownianItoIntegral` (integrand cut off after $T$, square integrable on every path) plus a pathwise $du$-integral of the drift on every path; reason: under the augmented filtration an integrand can be set to zero on the exceptional null set without losing measurability, so the classes agree up to indistinguishability
--   - reading: for $u\le v$, $C_v-C_u$ is adapted to the σ-algebra generated by $\{X_t,u\le t\le v\}$ (p.11) → $C_v-C_u$ is measurable with respect to $\sigma\{X_t:u\le t\le v\}$, for $u\le v\le T$; reason: "adapted" for a single random variable means measurable
--   - encoding: $Z_t=e^{-\int_0^t\eta_s dB_s-\frac12\int_0^t\eta_s^2ds}$ (12) → $Z_t=\exp(-I_t-\frac12\int_0^t\eta_u^2du)$ with $I$ a $\mathcal G$-adapted version of the Itô integral $\int\eta\,dB$; reason: an Itô integral is fixed only up to a null set; the paper uses that $Z$ is $\mathcal G$-adapted (p.ec23)
--   - encoding: "adapted" integrands $a,b,\gamma,\xi,\delta,\zeta$ → progressively measurable integrands; reason: the standard measurability of a stochastic integrand; on an augmented filtration an adapted process has a progressive modification
--   - encoding: identities of processes such as (EC.7.1), (18) → almost-sure equality for each $t\le T$; reason: the paper's equalities are between random variables at each time
--   - correction: $\delta_t(P,R)$ "increases in both $P$ and $R$" → increases in $P$, and in $R$ whenever $P\ge s$; reason: for $P<s$, $\tilde\sigma(P-s)<0$ while $\mathbb P^M(A_T\le R\mid\mathcal F_t)$ is strictly increasing in $R$ for $t<T$ (the independent Gaussian increment of $\tilde\sigma\tilde B$), so $\delta_t$ strictly decreases in $R$; every use is on $D$, where $P\ge c>s$
--   - reading: "increases" → both nondecreasing for $t\le T$ and strictly increasing for $t<T$; reason: the proof of Proposition 7 (p.ec48) uses that $\Psi(P,R)$ strictly increases; the stronger reading is stated
--   - reading: "In particular, $\delta_t(P,R)=\ldots$" (19) → every representing pair $(\xi,\delta)$ has $\delta$ equal to (19) $dt\otimes d\mathbb P$-a.e.; reason: the stronger reading; $\delta$ is unique because $\int\delta\,d\tilde B$ is orthogonal to $\int\xi\,dX$
--   - encoding: $V_t(P,R)=\mathbb E^M[H_T(P,R)\mid\mathcal F_t]$ (17), presupposed to exist → the hypothesis $\mathbb E^M[(C_T)^-]<\infty$; reason: otherwise the conditional expectation is a default $0$
--   - encoding: "martingale" (relative to $\mathcal F_t$, footnote 4) → `IsMartingaleOn`: adapted, integrable, and the martingale identity for $s\le t\le T$; reason: the paper's martingales live on $[0,T]$; Mathlib's `Martingale` constrains all times
-- source:
--   Wang–Yao–Zhang, How Does Risk Hedging Impact Operations? Insights from a Price-Setting Newsvendor Model, arXiv:2201.01026v4, Lemma 1, (17)–(19), p.15; proof §EC.7.2, pp.ec26–ec27

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_HedgePSNV_Model

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped NNReal ENNReal

namespace HedgePSNV

/-- Lemma 1, arXiv:2201.01026v4, p.15 (monotonicity clause corrected): suppose Assumptions
4–5. The projected production payoff `V_t(P,R) = E^M[H_T(P,R) | F_t]` of (17) has the
representation (18)
`V_t(P,R) = V_0(P,R) + ∫_0^t ξ_s(P,R) dX_s + ∫_0^t δ_s(P,R) dB̃_s`
with adapted `ξ`, `δ`, where `V_0(P,R) = E^M[H_T(P,R)]`. In particular, for every such
representation, `δ_t(P,R) = σ̃(P − s) P^M(A_T ≤ R | F_t)` (19) for almost every `t ∈ [0, T]`,
almost surely. This process increases in `P`, and in `R` whenever `P ≥ s`: weakly for every
`t ≤ T`, and strictly for `t < T` (in `R` strictly when `P > s`).

The four conjuncts are: existence of a representation; the formula (19) for every
representation; weak monotonicity for `t ≤ T`; strict monotonicity for `t < T`.

**Formalization Note** `ξ`, `δ` are progressively measurable; (18) holds almost surely for each
`t ≤ T`. The paper's "increases in both P and R" fails in `R` for `P < s` (then
`σ̃(P − s) < 0` and `P^M(A_T ≤ R | F_t)` is strictly increasing in `R` for `t < T`); on the
decision domain `P ≥ c > s`.
`NegPartIntegrable P^M` states that `E^M[H_T(P,R)]`, presupposed by (17), exists. -/
theorem lemma_1 (p : Params) {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (M : Model p Ω)
    (hM : M.Standing) (hint : M.NegPartIntegrable M.PM) (h4 : M.Assumption4)
    (h5 : M.Assumption5) :
    (∀ P R : ℝ, ∃ ξ δ χ L : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ, IsStronglyProgressive M.F ξ ∧
      IsStronglyProgressive M.F δ ∧ M.IsXGains ξ χ ∧ M.IsBtIntegral δ L ∧
      ∀ t : ℝ≥0, t ≤ p.T → M.V P R t =ᵐ[M.P] fun ω => M.V0 P R + χ t ω + L t ω) ∧
    (∀ P R : ℝ, ∀ ξ δ χ L : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ, IsStronglyProgressive M.F ξ →
      IsStronglyProgressive M.F δ → M.IsXGains ξ χ → M.IsBtIntegral δ L →
      (∀ t : ℝ≥0, t ≤ p.T → M.V P R t =ᵐ[M.P] fun ω => M.V0 P R + χ t ω + L t ω) →
      ∀ᵐ u ∂(volume.restrict (Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) (p.T : ℝ))),
        δ u.toNNReal =ᵐ[M.P] M.delta P R u.toNNReal) ∧
    (∀ t : ℝ≥0, t ≤ p.T →
      (∀ P P' R : ℝ, P ≤ P' → ∀ᵐ ω ∂M.P, M.delta P R t ω ≤ M.delta P' R t ω) ∧
      (∀ P R R' : ℝ, p.s ≤ P → R ≤ R' → ∀ᵐ ω ∂M.P, M.delta P R t ω ≤ M.delta P R' t ω)) ∧
    (∀ t : ℝ≥0, t < p.T →
      (∀ P P' R : ℝ, P < P' → ∀ᵐ ω ∂M.P, M.delta P R t ω < M.delta P' R t ω) ∧
      (∀ P R R' : ℝ, p.s < P → R < R' → ∀ᵐ ω ∂M.P, M.delta P R t ω < M.delta P R' t ω)) := by sorry

end HedgePSNV
